Sometimes the crust cracks along long breaks called faults. Huge blocks of rock can then be pushed up or slide down. Raised blocks form block mountains, often with one steep side and one gentle side.

The Sierra Nevada in California is a famous example. Its steep eastern face rises sharply above the desert. In India, the Satpura range in central India is often described as a block mountain, lifted between faults. The Narmada river flows west through a long, low valley that dropped between faults to the north of it.

{{story:himalaya-form}}

With a 70-metre rise, India's coasts would change shape. Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai and much of Gujarat's coast would be underwater. The sea would push far inland up the Ganga plain and cover most of Bangladesh.

Delhi, at about 200 metres above sea level, would stay dry, and Bengaluru, at over 900 metres, would be safe. In the USA, Florida would almost disappear. New York, Washington DC and Houston would flood. London, Shanghai and Venice would be lost.

{{story:sea-level-rise}}

In 1886, two young men working on opposite sides of the Atlantic found the same answer within weeks of each other. Charles Martin Hall in Ohio and Paul Héroult in France both used [[@electricity-made|electricity]] to pull aluminium out of its oxide.

Their method is still used. Aluminium oxide is [[dissolve|dissolved]] in a bath of molten mineral at about 950 °C, and a huge electric current passes through it. Pure aluminium collects at the bottom like a silver pool. Within a few years, the price had fallen to a small fraction of what it had been.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

When a petrol car brakes, its pads squeeze the wheels, and all that moving energy is wasted as heat. An electric car can do better. When the driver lifts off the pedal, the motor switches roles and becomes a generator. The wheels spin it, it makes electricity, and the battery soaks some of it up. This is called regenerative braking.

In stop-and-go city traffic, this can save a lot of energy. Some drivers barely use the brake pedal at all. Electric trains, including many of India's newer locomotives, use the same trick to feed power back into the overhead wires as they slow down.

{{story:batteries}}

Older and bigger scanners often use a CCD, or charge-coupled device. A set of mirrors and a lens shrinks the strip of page down onto a small sensor chip, much like the one inside a digital camera.

Many slim, cheap scanners use a CIS, or contact image sensor, instead. Here, a row of sensors as wide as the page sits almost touching the glass, lit by coloured LEDs. It needs no mirrors, so it's lighter and uses less power, though it struggles with books that don't lie flat.

{{story:pixels}}

Scrubbing matters as much as soap. Wash for at least 20 seconds, about as long as humming "Happy Birthday" twice, including between fingers and under nails.

The rubbing helps soap reach every crease and pulls germs out of the tiny grooves in your skin. Rinsing carries them off, and drying your hands on a clean towel removes even more. Warm water feels nicer, but cold water cleans just as well.

{{story:soap-history}}

India's RTI movement began in the dusty villages of central Rajasthan in the early 1990s. A group of workers and farmers called the Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan, co-founded by Aruna Roy, suspected that money for local works was being stolen.

They held *jan sunwais*, or public hearings. Records of roads, wells and wages were read aloud in front of the whole village. People stood up and said, "I never got that money" or "That wall was never built." The officials had little to say. These meetings made a simple demand catch on across India: if it's our money, show us the accounts. A national law followed in 2005.

{{story:journalism}}

The ancient Egyptians used fractions over 3,500 years ago. A scroll called the Rhind papyrus, now in the British Museum, is full of maths problems, like how to share loaves among workers. Egyptian scribes mostly used unit fractions, which have 1 on top, like 1/2 or 1/5. To write 3/4, they wrote 1/2 + 1/4.

This sounds clumsy, but it was practical for dividing bread. Share 5 loaves among 8 people by giving each person 1/2 + 1/8 of a loaf. Records from [[@ancient-egypt|ancient Egypt]] show workers on great building projects were paid partly in bread and beer, so fair shares really mattered. Getting a fraction wrong could mean a hungry worker, or an angry crew.

{{story:ancient-egypt}}

Madhava's best-known result says that pi divided by 4 equals 1 − 1/3 + 1/5 − 1/7 + 1/9, and so on, forever. In Europe, James Gregory and Gottfried Leibniz found it in the 1670s, and it was long called the Leibniz series. Many books now call it the Madhava–Leibniz series.

There is a catch. The series gets close to pi painfully slowly. Even after 100 terms, the answer for pi is still off by about 0.01. Madhava found a smart fix: a correction term added at the end that makes a short sum far more accurate. Using methods like this, he calculated pi as 3.14159265359, correct to 11 decimal places.

{{story:astronomy-ancient-india}}

एक बड़े के शरीर के वज़न का लगभग आधे से 60 प्रतिशत तक हिस्सा पानी है, और नन्हे बच्चों में तो और भी ज़्यादा। तुम्हारा दिमाग, मांसपेशियाँ, [[@kidneys|गुर्दे]] और खून ज़्यादातर पानी ही हैं। हड्डियों में भी कुछ पानी होता है।

पानी खाने के [[nutrient|पोषक तत्वों]] और ऑक्सीजन को पूरे शरीर में पहुँचाता है, पसीने के ज़रिए ठंडक देता है, और जोड़ों को आराम से हिलने में मदद करता है। गुर्दे इससे गंदगी बाहर बहाते हैं। एक सेहतमंद बड़ा रोज़ लगभग दो से तीन लीटर पानी गँवाता है, और राजस्थान या अमेरिका के एरिज़ोना जैसी जगह पर किसी गर्म दिन में कहीं ज़्यादा।

{{story:camels}}

At first, emojis from one Japanese phone company often showed up as nonsense on another company's phones. The fix came from [[@unicode|Unicode]], a system that gives every letter and symbol in the world's writing systems its own number. In 2010, hundreds of emojis were added to Unicode, so phones and computers everywhere could agree on them.

When Apple added an emoji keyboard to iPhones worldwide in 2011, emojis took off. By 2015, Oxford Dictionaries chose the "face with tears of joy" emoji as its Word of the Year. There are now almost 4,000 emojis, counting different skin tones and combinations.

{{story:hieroglyphs}}

[[@voice|Your voice]] is a pattern of [[vibration|vibrations]] in the air, called sound waves. A microphone turns them into numbers. The computer slices those numbers into tiny pieces, about a hundredth of a second each, and studies the mix of high and low pitches in each slice.

A speech model, trained on thousands of hours of people talking, works out which speech sounds those slices probably are, and which words they probably make. It also uses context. If you say "play some music by", it expects a singer's name next, not a vegetable.

{{story:ears-hear}}

Around 1980, a team including the scientist Yash Pal studied pictures from the American Landsat [[@satellites-india|satellites]]. They traced long, pale bands across the Thar region: the ghosts of old river channels, buried under sand and fields. Later work by ISRO mapped these channels in more detail.

Some wells dug along the old courses strike water deep underground. The land itself seems to remember where the river ran.

{{story:rivers-himalaya}}

In 1519, Hernán Cortés landed on the coast of Mexico with about 500 soldiers. He soon learned that many peoples resented the Aztecs, who demanded heavy tribute from them. The Tlaxcalans and others joined him in large numbers. A young Indigenous woman known as Malinche, who spoke several languages, became his interpreter and adviser.

Cortés entered [[@aztec-tenochtitlan|Tenochtitlan]], the Aztec capital, as a guest of the emperor Moctezuma II, then took him prisoner. Fighting broke out. In 1520 a smallpox [[epidemic|epidemic]] swept through the city. After a long siege, Tenochtitlan fell on 13 August 1521.

{{story:smallpox}}

In 1851, Anthony met Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who had helped organise the Seneca Falls Convention, the first big women's rights meeting in the US, in 1848. The two became close friends and partners for the next 50 years.

Stanton, a mother of seven, was the brilliant writer. Anthony, who never married, was the organiser and traveller. Stanton once said that she forged the thunderbolts and Anthony fired them. Together, they founded the National Woman Suffrage Association in 1869.

{{story:womens-vote}}

Vedic people prayed to gods linked with nature. Indra was the storm god who brought rain and won battles. Agni was fire, the messenger who carried offerings up to the other gods. Varuna watched over order in the world, and Soma was a sacred drink as well as a god.

Worship centred on the *yajna*, a fire ritual where priests poured ghee and grain into the flames while chanting. Some hymns ask big questions too. One famous hymn about creation ends by wondering whether even the highest god knows how the world began.

{{story:sanskrit}}

Below Haridwar, the river slows down. It now flows through the Indo-Gangetic plain, a vast stretch of soft soil laid down by Himalayan rivers over millions of years. This soil is why so much wheat, rice and sugarcane grows here.

At Prayagraj the Yamuna joins it, and every twelve years crowds gather there for the Kumbh Mela. Further east is [[@varanasi|Varanasi]], where steps called ghats run down to the water for about 6 km. In Bihar, rivers such as the Gandak and the Kosi pour in from Nepal. The Kosi changes its course so often that it has earned the name "Sorrow of Bihar".

{{story:water-cycle}}

*Chandamama*, meaning "moon uncle", was launched in Madras in July 1947, a month before independence, by the film producers B. Nagi Reddi and Chakrapani. It began in Telugu and Tamil and soon appeared in many other languages.

Its most loved series was about King Vikram and the Vetala, a spirit hanging from a tree. Each month Vikram carried it off on his shoulder, and each month it told him a story ending in a riddle. [[@chandamama|Chandamama]] drew on the [[@panchatantra-tales|Panchatantra]], Jataka tales and old epics, with rich painted pictures. The artist K. C. Sivasankaran, known as Sankar, drew Vikram and the Vetala for decades, and his gloomy tree and grim-faced king became famous. It faded in the 2000s and stopped publishing around 2013.

{{story:comics-india}}

"Asha was scared" tells the reader something. "Asha's hands were shaking so much she dropped the key twice" shows it. Showing lets readers work things out for themselves, which makes them feel part of the story.

Small, concrete details help too. Instead of "a nice house", try "a pink house with a mango tree that dropped fruit onto the neighbour's car". [[@ruskin-bond|Ruskin Bond]] is a master of this. In his stories, you can almost smell the rain on the pine trees of Mussoorie.

{{story:brain-works}}

Kabir grew up in a family of Muslim weavers in Varanasi, one of the holiest cities for Hindus. He worked at a loom, and [[weaving|weaving]] shows up again and again in his poems.

He refused to take sides. He mocked empty [[ritual|rituals]] of both Hindus and Muslims, from [[pilgrimage|pilgrimages]] to fasting, and said God was inside every person. That made him enemies among priests of both faiths. It also made him popular with weavers, farmers and labourers.

{{story:varanasi}}

दक्षिण अमेरिका के लोग 5,000 साल से भी पहले कोको इस्तेमाल कर रहे थे। बाद में मध्य अमेरिका के [[@aztecs-maya|माया और एज़्टेक]] लोग इसे एक झागदार, कड़वे पेय की तरह पीते थे, कभी-कभी मिर्च डालकर। कोको के बीज इतने क़ीमती थे कि उन्हें पैसे की तरह चलाया जाता था।

स्पेन के खोजी यात्री 1500 के दशक में कोको यूरोप ले गए। वहाँ लोगों ने इसमें चीनी मिलाई और इसे गर्म-गर्म पीने लगे। चॉकलेट की ठोस बार तो बहुत बाद में, 1800 के दशक में आई।

{{story:aztecs-maya}}

The nutrition table lists energy in kilocalories (kcal), along with protein, [[carbohydrate|carbohydrate]], sugar, fat and sodium, which comes mostly from salt. American labels, updated in 2016, also show "added sugars" on their own line, separate from the sugar found naturally in milk or fruit.

Comparing two foods is easiest using the per-100 g column, because servings can be different sizes. A packet with 30 g of sugar per 100 g is almost one-third sugar. Understanding [[@percentages|percentages]] is half the skill here. The other half is noticing how small some "servings" are, and whether the packet holds three of them.

{{story:percentages}}

Before it was a snack, Sandwich was a small port town in Kent, in southeast England. The name comes from Old English and means something like a sandy harbour or market town. The Montagu family took their title from it in 1660.

The 4th Earl's name also travelled across the Pacific. As head of the navy, he supported Captain James Cook's voyages. In 1778, Cook reached a group of islands and named them the Sandwich Islands in his honour. Today, those islands are [[@hawaii-culture|Hawaii]], which has its own name, from its own people.

{{story:hawaii-culture}}

The grandest banana-leaf meal is Kerala's sadya, served at weddings and especially at the festival of [[@onam-pongal|Onam]]. A sadya often has more than twenty items. They include avial (mixed vegetables in coconut and curd), olan, kalan, banana chips, sharkara upperi ([[@jaggery|jaggery]]-coated banana chips), pickles, pappadam and two or more kinds of payasam.

{{story:onam-pongal}}

The leaf is laid with its narrow tip to the diner's left. Side dishes are placed in a set position on the upper half, and rice is served on the lower half. In many homes, folding the leaf towards you after eating means you enjoyed the meal.
